Feature: slice-6 / l10n-parity-ci
Branch: feat/web-app-slice-6
Verified: 2026-05-28
Anchored to: docs/18 §13.11 ("CI gate failing when pl.json and en.json drift")

What was built
==============
A zero-dependency Node script (no jq, no extra npm packages) that
walks marketing-site/messages/pl.json and en.json, flattens both
into dotted-key sets, and reports any divergence.

Behaviour:
  - Exit 0 when key sets are equal.
  - Exit 1 when either side has a key the other doesn't, with a
    list of the missing keys per locale on stderr.
  - Emits a non-blocking WARNING for keys whose values are
    byte-for-byte identical across locales — common copy-paste
    omission. A SHARED_VALUE_ALLOWLIST suppresses legitimately
    shared values (brand name, NIP/VAT codes, plan tier names,
    placeholder formats, copyright line).

Wiring
======
- marketing-site/scripts/check-l10n-parity.mjs (new) — the script.
- marketing-site/package.json — adds:
    "check:l10n": "node scripts/check-l10n-parity.mjs"
    "ci": "pnpm run check:l10n && pnpm run lint && pnpm run build"
- .github/workflows/marketing-site.yml — new "l10n parity (PL ↔ EN)"
  step in the build job, between lint and build. Fails the marketing-
  site CI workflow on drift so PRs can't merge with mismatched
  locale files.

Verification
============
Initial state (no drift):
  $ pnpm run check:l10n
  > l10n parity OK — 413 keys per locale.
  $ echo $?
  0

Drift detection (canary key injected into pl.json only):
  $ node scripts/check-l10n-parity.mjs
  > Missing in en.json (1):
  >   - __drift_test__
  > l10n parity check FAILED.
  > pl.json keys: 414  en.json keys: 413
  $ echo $?
  1

The drift-test fixture was rolled back; current keystore is in
sync (413 keys per locale, no warnings under the allowlist).

Why a Node script and not jq
============================
- jq's recursive flatten across nested objects requires a clumsy
  reduce + path() walk; a 60-line ES module is clearer and gets
  syntax highlighting in editors.
- Zero new npm deps — the script uses only node:fs / node:path /
  node:url.
- Same Node 20 the rest of the CI workflow already provisions,
  so no setup overhead.

